# React & Next.js Component Patterns

## Server vs Client Boundary
```typescript
// ✅ Default: Server Component — no directive needed
export default async function ProfilePage({ params }) { ... }

// ✅ Only add 'use client' when you need browser APIs, event handlers, or hooks
'use client';
export function ProfileEditForm() { ... }
```
- Keep Server Components as deep as possible; push `'use client'` to leaf nodes.
- Never use `useEffect` to fetch data — use Server Components or TanStack Query.

## Component Structure (top → bottom)
```typescript
// 1. Imports
// 2. Types / interfaces local to this file
// 3. Component function (named export preferred)
// 4. Sub-components or helpers used only here
// 5. Default export at the bottom
```

## Composition over Prop Drilling
```typescript
// ❌ BAD — prop drilling 3+ levels
<Layout user={user} theme={theme} onLogout={onLogout} />

// ✅ GOOD — slot / compound pattern
<Layout>
  <Layout.Header><UserMenu /></Layout.Header>
  <Layout.Body>{children}</Layout.Body>
</Layout>
```

## Custom Hooks
- One hook = one concern. Name: `use<Domain><Action>` → `useProfileUpdate`, `useSnippetSearch`.
- Return a stable object shape: `{ data, isLoading, error, refetch }`.
- Keep side effects inside hooks, not inside components.

## Performance
- `React.memo` only for proven re-render bottlenecks — measure first.
- Wrap expensive computations with `useMemo`; stable callbacks with `useCallback` when passed to memoized children.
- Use `Suspense` + `loading.tsx` for async boundaries in the App Router.
